#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431354 :question/problem "![](images/65/65047f9bf4f035000175fc55ee3ba6eeb4aa7ec39674a1984b95ba98a3f754f6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=13x.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(13-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (18-4) \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431355 :question/problem "![](images/db/dbe6009d42623d3b3cd9f05380159bb0f4b8f22b920c1ef5d4d82b5dbf152d4a.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y(x+1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6xy+2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6xy+2y)-2y(x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-0)\\cdot (4-1)-(4-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431356 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9be396fd57559bb77f92d5a0421f3a7cdca577203904e577da0caf5326caac91.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x$ and $Q(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-8{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))\\cdot (16-16)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431357 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=7,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-3,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433352 :question/problem "![](images/03/0353d9a2b8c8e46552e0816658628f67b58c0c6bef51bd05261becac42a55798.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,-2<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{t+2}\\right)}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{0}(0+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1)(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780 :question/problem "![](images/37/37a6eb13f8a6f6ab46040495894dedeec4951b4814aad19f45bfbd4ed7040767.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433781 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fca2d0c37c8831a66c3c183b31b84b41fd5a16740ff13fd86f4877bdaeb60a6.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/10/1060c071df1dbd197c14da529819a34bd4457868fdd4cbe78a7f93f12dbf67da.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (2-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[(8\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434277 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{13}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=13$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=13,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{13}=2\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434278 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2adb84964a605495c84921943ec76f9820ba18851cda817037dd6022808d1fd7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434279 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434830 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}yz⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,xz,\\,yz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =v\\cdot 2u+uv\\cdot (-1)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2uv-uv+0 \\\\  & =uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot uv+0\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+0=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434831 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-u-v)+(v-u)\\cdot (v-u)+(-uv)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-2uv-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-2uv+{u}^{2}-2uv \\\\  & =-6uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{-2}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435806 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<\\frac{1}{y-x}<1 \\\\ y-x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435807 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $x+y\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡g(x,y)$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $x+y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435808 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nxy\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435809 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435810 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}$ at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} & =\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436026 :question/problem "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(-2,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,-1) & =4{(-2)}^{2}+9{(-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4(4)+9(1) \\\\  & =16+9 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the $25$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=25.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436027 :question/problem "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{2}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4 & =x-y \\\\ y & =x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the line $y=x-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436028 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [0,\\infty ),$ the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436123 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,-3,2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436125 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436126 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436127 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436128 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436129 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436130 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436604 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\cdot 0-2y) \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,\\sqrt{\\pi })} & =4(\\sqrt{\\pi })\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436605 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3xy-1}{2x-3y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{3xy-1}{2x-3y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-1)\\cdot (2x-3y)-(3xy-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3y)}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x\\cdot (2x-3y)-(3xy-1)\\cdot (-3)}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-9xy+9xy-3}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-3}{(2x-3y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(0,-1)} & =\\frac{6(0)^{2}-3}{(2(0)-3(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436606 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y){e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})\\cdot xy-{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)}{(xy)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\cdot xy-{e}^{xy}\\cdot y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot xy-y{e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}(xy-1)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(xy-1){e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=xy-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436607 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(4,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-{z}^{4}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (-{z}^{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{z}^{4}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(4,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}{|}_{(4,1,1)} & =-(1)^{4}{e}^{4(1)-4(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436608 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=(2y+1)^{2x+1},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((2y+1)^{2x+1}) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}((2y+1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot (2\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot (2\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =2(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,0)} & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2+1)\\cdot (2\\cdot 0+1)^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot {1}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436609 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule and the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) & ={y}^{2}(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}})) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}((2x)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (2x)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}(x+{x}^{3}){e}^{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436610 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436611 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product and the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot (4{y}^{3}))) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436612 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)-xy.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)-xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx) & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot 1-y\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436613 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2y\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,1)} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(1)^{2}}{2(1)\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+3}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436614 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6x+11y)\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(11\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{22y-(6x+11y)}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{11y-6x}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=11y-6x.\n$$"}]}
